{"id":"ed71f8d5-cdfc-419f-b429-798639833279","object":"chat.completion","created":1785024825,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    if max_rounds == 0:\\n        return {\\\"applications\\\": [], \\\"fixed_point\\\": True, \\\"text\\\": text}\\n    applications = []\\n    changed = True\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            if p[\\\"old\\\"] in text:\\n                text = text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n                applications.append(p[\\\"name\\\"])\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            break\\n    fixed_point = not changed\\n    return {\\\"applications\\\": applications, \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point, \\\"text\\\": text}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the task. We need to implement solve(case) for SE-CR-01. The input case structure based on fixtures: payload has \"text\", \"passes\" (list of objects with name, old, new), \"max_rounds\", \"_case_nonce\" (ignore). The output must be an object with \"applications\" (array of strings), \"fixed_point\" (boolean), \"text\" (string). The function should simulate applying passes repeatedly up to max_rounds times, tracking applications.\n\nInterpretation: We have a text and a list of passes. Each pass is a substitution: replace all occurrences of \"old\" with \"new\". However, we need to apply passes in order? The problem likely is from Effectslice: \"SE-CR-01\" suggests a string rewriting task. The fixture shows three identical payloads? Actually the payloads are identical but with different nonces. But the task is to implement solve. The output schema uses \"applications\", \"fixed_point\", \"text\". Likely \"applications\" is a list of pass names that were applied in each round? Or list of pass names in order of application? The fixture doesn't provide expected outputs, but we can infer from typical pattern: We simulate rounds. In each round, apply each pass in order? Or possibly apply passes sequentially until no further changes? \"max_rounds\" limits number of rounds. The output \"text\" after simulation, \"fixed_point\" indicates whether further changes would occur (i.e., after max_rounds or when no passes change text). \"applications\" likely list of pass names applied in each round? Or a list of all applications across all rounds? The schema says items are strings, array. Possibly it's a list of names of passes that were applied (i.e., that actually changed the text) in order of application? Or per round? Let's think.\n\nGiven the fixture payloads are identical, the solve function should produce deterministic result. Since no expected output given, we must produce a correct solution based on typical rewriting task. I recall from Effectslice documentation that often \"applications\" lists the names of passes that had an effect in each round, perhaps flattened. But we need to be careful.\n\nAnother possibility: The task is to simulate the rewriting process as described in the problem statement of the task. Since we only have the structural schema and the fixture, we must infer logic. Typically, for such tasks: we have a \"text\", a list of passes (each with name, old, new), and max_rounds. We apply the passes sequentially in order from first to last, each pass replaces all occurrences of \"old\" with \"new\". After applying all passes, that's one round. We repeat for up to max_rounds, but stop early if no pass changes anything in a round (i.e., fixed point). The output \"applications\" is a list of pass names that were applied (i.e., that caused at least one replacement) in each round, in the order they were applied. Or maybe it's the list of all applications across all rounds in sequence. The schema says \"items\": {\"type\": \"string\"}, so it's a flat array of strings.\n\nLet's examine typical Examples from Effectslice tasks: I've seen similar tasks like \"SE-CR-01\" in the Effectslice dataset. It's a \"String rewriting\" task. Usually, the output \"applications\" is a list of pass names that were applied (i.e., that changed the string) during the process, in the order they were applied, across all rounds. And \"fixed_point\" is True if after the final round no pass changed the string, else False (if max_rounds reached and still changing). \"text\" is the final string.\n\nGiven that there is \"max_rounds\", we should simulate at most max_rounds rounds. In each round, we iterate through passes in order. For each pass, we check if the old string appears in current text. If it does, we replace all occurrences (using string.replace(old, new) perhaps? But careful: If old and new are overlapping? Typically simple substring replace. Also need to consider that passes are applied sequentially, so later passes see the result of earlier ones in the same round. We continue for each round until either we've done max_rounds rounds, or a round where no pass made any change (then stop). Then return the final text, whether fixed_point (whether we stopped due to no changes) and the list of applications (names of passes that actually did a replacement, in order of application). The fixture shows passes have names \"halve-a\", \"drop-b\", \"drop-c\". For the given text \"aaaa bb cc\", let's simulate:\n\nRound 1:\n- halve-a: old \"aa\", new \"a\". All occurrences of \"aa\" to \"a\". Original \"aaaa\" -> replace first \"aa\" to \"a\" -> \"aa bb cc\"? Actually \"aaaa\" contains overlapping \"aa\"'s. Python's str.replace replaces non-overlapping occurrences left to right. So \"aaaa\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") gives \"aa\" because it replaces first two \"aa\" -> \"a\", then remaining \"aa\" -> \"a\", result \"aa\". So text becomes \"aa bb cc\". Applied.\n- drop-b: old \"bb\", new \"b\". \"aa bb cc\" -> replace \"bb\" with \"b\" gives \"aa b cc\". Applied.\n- drop-c: old \" cc\", new \"\". \"aa b cc\" -> replace \" cc\" with \"\" gives \"aa b\". Applied.\nEnd of round 1. Applications: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\"] (all applied). text = \"aa b\".\n\nRound 2:\n- halve-a: \"aa b\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") -> \"a b\". Applied.\n- drop-b: \"a b\".replace(\"bb\",\"b\") -> no \"bb\", so not applied. (check: \"a b\" has no \"bb\")\n- drop-c: \"a b\".replace(\" cc\",\"\") -> no \" cc\", not applied.\nSo round 2 applications: [\"halve-a\"] only. text = \"a b\".\n\nRound 3:\n- halve-a: \"a b\".replace(\"aa\",\"a\") -> no \"aa\", not applied.\n- drop-b: no \"bb\", not applied.\n- drop-c: no \" cc\", not applied.\nNo changes in round 3, so fixed point reached after round 2? Actually after round 2, we had changes. Then round 3 no changes, so we stop. If max_rounds is 8, we stop after round 3 because fixed point. So final text \"a b\". fixed_point = True. applications list would be across all rounds: [\"halve-a\",\"drop-b\",\"drop-c\",\"halve-a\"]. That is 4 applications.\n\nBut the output schema only says \"applications\" is array of strings. Could be that we include only passes that actually did something, in order across rounds. That seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to implement solve(case). The case argument is the payload object (without outer case_id etc). So we expect case to have keys \"text\", \"passes\", \"max_rounds\", and possibly \"_case_nonce\". We ignore nonce.\n\nImplementation steps:\n- Initialize current_text = case[\"text\"]\n- Initialize applications = [] (list of strings)\n- For round in range(max_rounds):\n   - changed_in_round = False\n   - For each pass in case[\"passes\"]:\n        name = pass[\"name\"]\n        old = pass[\"old\"]\n        new = pass[\"new\"]\n        # Count occurrences? But we just check if old in text.\n        if old in current_text:\n            current_text = current_text.replace(old, new)\n            applications.append(name)\n            changed_in_round = True\n   - If not changed_in_round: break\n- fixed_point = (not changed_in_round) after loop? Actually if we break because no change, then fixed_point = True. If we exhaust max_rounds and still had changes in last round, then fixed_point = False.\n- Return dict: {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": current_text}\n\nBut note: In round where changes occurred, after finishing that round, we next round. If we break because no change, we exit and fixed_point should be True. If loop completes all max_rounds without early break, then fixed_point is False (because last round had changes, otherwise would have broken). So we can set fixed_point = False initially, then if we break early, set to True.\n\nEdge cases: passes might have overlapping or contradictory replacements. But that's fine.\n\nNow produce source code. Must be Python standard library only. Write solve function. Use typing? Not required.\n\nWe must output as JSON with \"implementation\" containing the Python source as a string. Escape properly.\n\nLet's write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    applications = []\n    fixed_point = False\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p[\"name\"]\n            old = p[\"old\"]\n            new = p[\"new\"]\n            if old in text:\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(name)\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            fixed_point = True\n            break\n    else:\n        fixed_point = False  # loop exhausted without break, so last round had changes\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nBut note: If we break because changed==False, we already set fixed_point=True. If we finish loop normally (i.e., no break), then after loop we set fixed_point=False. But we need to ensure that if max_rounds=0? Possibly, but max_rounds likely positive. We can handle safely.\n\nAlternatively: We can track changed flag and after loop check: fixed_point = not changed (if we break) or if exhausted loop then fixed_point = False (since changes occurred in last round). But if max_rounds=0, then no rounds, fixed_point? Probably True because no changes? But not in fixtures.\n\nBetter: Simulate rounds until either no change or max_rounds reached. Then after loop, fixed_point is True if we stopped because no change, else False. We can implement with while loop with round counter.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    applications = []\n    rounds = 0\n    while rounds < max_rounds:\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            old = p[\"old\"]\n            new = p[\"new\"]\n            if old in text:\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(p[\"name\"])\n                changed = True\n        rounds += 1\n        if not changed:\n            break\n    fixed_point = not changed if rounds <= max_rounds else False? Actually after loop, if we break because not changed, then fixed_point=True. If we exit because rounds==max_rounds, we need to check if the last round had changes. Variable 'changed' holds the status of the last round. So after loop, fixed_point = (changed == False). But careful: If max_rounds > 0 and we never enter loop (max_rounds=0), then changed is undefined. So handle separately.\n\nSimplify: Keep track of any change in the last executed round. Use a variable changed_in_last_round. After loop, fixed_point = not changed_in_last_round.\n\nImplementation:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    applications = []\n    changed_in_last_round = True  # dummy initial\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            name = p[\"name\"]\n            old = p[\"old\"]\n            new = p[\"new\"]\n            if old in text:\n                text = text.replace(old, new)\n                applications.append(name)\n                changed = True\n        changed_in_last_round = changed\n        if not changed:\n            break\n    fixed_point = not changed_in_last_round\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nThis works for max_rounds>=0. If max_rounds=0, loop not executed, changed_in_last_round remains True, so fixed_point=False, but there were no changes. That's arguably wrong, but max_rounds is given as positive (8) in fixture. We can add handling: if max_rounds==0, then text unchanged, no applications, fixed_point=True. But to be safe, we can handle:\n\nif max_rounds == 0:\n    return {\"applications\": [], \"fixed_point\": True, \"text\": text}\n\nBut the task likely expects max_rounds>0. We'll include for completeness.\n\nNow produce final answer.\n\nWe must output a JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source as a string, escaped.\n\nLet's write the source code as a string. Use triple quotes? But need to escape newlines and quotes. We'll build the string.\n\nImplementation code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    text = case[\"text\"]\n    passes = case[\"passes\"]\n    max_rounds = case[\"max_rounds\"]\n    if max_rounds == 0:\n        return {\"applications\": [], \"fixed_point\": True, \"text\": text}\n    applications = []\n    changed = True  # dummy\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\n        changed = False\n        for p in passes:\n            if p[\"old\"] in text:\n                text = text.replace(p[\"old\"], p[\"new\"])\n                applications.append(p[\"name\"])\n                changed = True\n        if not changed:\n            break\n    fixed_point = not changed\n    return {\"applications\": applications, \"fixed_point\": fixed_point, \"text\": text}\n\nWe can also use name variable, but it's fine.\n\nNow produce JSON. The entire response must be a JSON object with one key \"implementation\". The value is the source code string.\n\nLet's format:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    text = case[\\\"text\\\"]\\n    passes = case[\\\"passes\\\"]\\n    max_rounds = case[\\\"max_rounds\\\"]\\n    if max_rounds == 0:\\n        return {\\\"applications\\\": [], \\\"fixed_point\\\": True, \\\"text\\\": text}\\n    applications = []\\n    changed = True\\n    for _ in range(max_rounds):\\n        changed = False\\n        for p in passes:\\n            if p[\\\"old\\\"] in text:\\n                text = text.replace(p[\\\"old\\\"], p[\\\"new\\\"])\\n                applications.append(p[\\\"name\\\"])\\n                changed = True\\n        if not changed:\\n            break\\n    fixed_point = not changed\\n    return {\\\"applications\\\": applications, \\\"fixed_point\\\": fixed_point, \\\"text\\\": text}\"\n}\n\nWe need to ensure that backslashes and quotes are properly escaped in JSON. The source code contains double quotes inside, so we escape them as \\\". Also newlines as \\n. That's fine.\n\nLet's produce final answer."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1037,"completion_tokens":3527,"total_tokens":4564,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":3322},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":13},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}